    thinking: "**Defining React State Variables**\n\nI\'ve decided to start building the React front-end. My initial focus is on defining the necessary React state variables. So far, I\'ve outlined the need for `documentImage`, which will hold the current document as a base64 string or null. Additionally, `fileName` will track the current file\'s name, defaulting to \"TAMIL_PRE_ii.PNG\".\n\n\n**Expanding State Definitions**\n\nI\'ve expanded the state variable definitions further. I\'ve added `isPreloadedSample` for the preloaded sample page display. The `transcription` string and the `languages` array now store OCR output and detected languages. I\'m also including `confidence`, `sections`, `isAnalyzing`, `errorMessage`, `viewMode`, `clipboardStatus`, and `stats`. I also need a Preloaded Tamil page rendering component to display the original document if there is no user uploaded file.\n\n\n**Visualizing Preloaded State**\n\nI\'m now focused on the visual representation of the preloaded state. I\'m leaning towards rendering a mockup of a book page to meet the \"Artistic Flair\" design directive and provide a user friendly display. This involves a parchment-colored card, a vintage-style \"vii\" heading, neat Tamil text lines, and a red scanline animation. I am thinking about how this reflects the idea of the page as a map of intent.\nI am also checking if any other files need reading or replacing, such as `/src/App.tsx`. I am also examining the current use of `framer-motion` and motion libraries to ensure their usage for animations, as per the framework rules.\n\n\n**Designing the Sample Page**\n\nI\'m now diving into the design of the preloaded Tamil page. I\'m importing `motion` for animations and planning to use it in the app\'s `App.tsx`. I\'ve decided to create a high-fidelity styled document within a parchment-colored box, complete with double rules, a \"vii\" title, and formatted Tamil text. This will evoke the feel of a real archive reading room, using an off-white neo-brutalist theme for the master applet frame.\n\n\n**Refining Container Layout**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the overall layout. I\'m aiming for a fully responsive design using `max-w-7xl mx-auto flex-1 flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 p-6`, ensuring it\'s modular and handles both desktop and mobile screens gracefully. The goal is a neat container that matches the Artistic Flair specification, supports uploads, and enables immediate execution. The components include a header (`h-24` with specific styling), a custom title, a monospaced subtitle, a status sidebar, a vertical timeline navigation bar (20 wide), and a bottom footer status bar.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now implement the full frontend and client state of our document text extractor in `src/App.tsx`. I will write a custom paper simulation card for the user\'s uploaded book page (complete with original Tamil typesetting, dotted leaders, and pagination matching the photo precisely) as an interactive preloaded sample. Any file uploaded gets processed via the server-side Gemini OCR engine, styled with Space Grotesk display headings, neon scan swept lines, and Playfair Display serif layouts conforming to the Artistic Flair theme."
